          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "Unrestricted File Upload on /file-upload Endpoint",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted File Upload on /file-upload Endpoint"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `/file-upload` endpoint accepts file uploads without any validation of file extensions, MIME types, or content. PHP, executable, and other dangerous file types are accepted and stored without restriction."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `/file-upload` endpoint accepts file uploads without any validation of file extensions, MIME types, or content. PHP, executable, and other dangerous file types are accepted and stored without restriction.\n\nArbitrary file upload enables remote code execution if uploaded files are accessible and executed by the server. Malicious PHP shells, executables, and other code can be deployed to the application server.\n\n1. Implement whitelist-based file extension validation on the server side (not client-side)\n2. Validate MIME types by inspecting file magic bytes, not Content-Type headers\n3. Store uploaded files outside the web root or in a directory with no execute permissions\n4. Rename uploaded files with a random UUID to prevent direct access\n5. Implement file size limits\n6. Add authentication requirement to file upload endpoints\n7. Scan uploaded files with antivirus/malware detection\n8. Disable script execution in upload directories via web server configuration",
                "markdown": "The `/file-upload` endpoint accepts file uploads without any validation of file extensions, MIME types, or content. PHP, executable, and other dangerous file types are accepted and stored without restriction.\n\nArbitrary file upload enables remote code execution if uploaded files are accessible and executed by the server. Malicious PHP shells, executables, and other code can be deployed to the application server.\n\n1. Implement whitelist-based file extension validation on the server side (not client-side)\n2. Validate MIME types by inspecting file magic bytes, not Content-Type headers\n3. Store uploaded files outside the web root or in a directory with no execute permissions\n4. Rename uploaded files with a random UUID to prevent direct access\n5. Implement file size limits\n6. Add authentication requirement to file upload endpoints\n7. Scan uploaded files with antivirus/malware detection\n8. Disable script execution in upload directories via web server configuration"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
